WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
In the copying video we saw billions of

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.680
letters packed into a nucleus.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:05.440
How?

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.240
If you could unwind the DNA in just

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.400
one of your cells, you would have a

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.520
thread about two metres long.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.360
It sits in a nucleus about six thousandths

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.720
of a millimetre across.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.920
Blow that nucleus up to the size of

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.960
a tennis ball, and the thread would be

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.360
more than ten kilometres long.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.000
So how does a cell store DNA this

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.880
long and still use it?

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.480
And does a bacterium, which has no nucleus

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.240
at all, do it differently?

00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:36.586
Every cell stores its genes in DNA, the

00:00:36.707 --> 00:00:38.346
same double helix you met earlier.

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:41.546
What differs is where the DNA is kept

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:43.146
and what shape it takes.

00:00:43.787 --> 00:00:46.826
A eukaryote, like an animal or plant cell,

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:49.866
keeps its DNA inside a nucleus, an inner

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:51.946
compartment with a membrane around it.

00:00:52.507 --> 00:00:54.746
A prokaryote has no nucleus.

00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:57.626
Bacteria are the prokaryotes we will use as

00:00:57.707 --> 00:00:58.586
our example.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.240
Start with a bacterium.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.760
In most bacteria the main DNA is one

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.720
long molecule whose two ends are joined, so

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.880
it forms a ring, which we call a

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.800
circular chromosome.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.280
With no nucleus around it, the ring lies

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.200
in one region of the cell called the

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.960
nucleoid, a word that means nucleus-like.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.280
The nucleoid has no membrane.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.800
It is simply the part of the cell

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.400
where the DNA sits.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.080
This ring is far longer than the cell.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.560
Stretched out, it would be about one and

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.000
a half millimetres, several hundred times the length

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:35.720
of the cell.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.240
So it is not floating loose.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.120
Enzymes twist the ring, the way twisting a

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.160
loop of rubber band makes it coil up

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.280
on itself, and proteins grip the coiled ring

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.600
at many points to hold it compact.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.720
That packs the long ring into the nucleoid

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.320
without any membrane.

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:58.853
Many bacteria also carry plasmids: small, separate rings

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.733
of DNA, usually much smaller than the main

00:02:01.814 --> 00:02:02.533
chromosome.

00:02:03.174 --> 00:02:05.413
The cell copies a plasmid separately from the

00:02:05.494 --> 00:02:08.613
main chromosome, and a plasmid often carries extra

00:02:08.774 --> 00:02:11.173
genes, such as one that makes the bacterium

00:02:11.294 --> 00:02:13.493
resistant to an antibiotic.

00:02:13.574 --> 00:02:15.973
You saw bacteria pass DNA to each other

00:02:16.054 --> 00:02:17.573
in conjugation.

00:02:17.654 --> 00:02:20.053
Plasmids are often what they pass on.

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:23.106
Now a eukaryotic cell.

00:02:23.507 --> 00:02:26.306
Its DNA is inside the nucleus, and it

00:02:26.387 --> 00:02:28.066
is not one ring.

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:31.026
It is split into several chromosomes, and each

00:02:31.107 --> 00:02:34.306
is one DNA molecule with two ends, so

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.146
we call them linear.

00:02:36.187 --> 00:02:38.786
In a human body cell you counted 46

00:02:38.827 --> 00:02:40.226
in mitosis.

00:02:40.547 --> 00:02:43.506
Together they hold those two metres of DNA,

00:02:43.747 --> 00:02:46.546
about six billion pairs of bases.

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.786
That is why the packing matters so much

00:02:48.907 --> 00:02:49.186
here.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.200
A nucleus holds that much DNA because the

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.400
thread is wound and folded.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.920
Loose, two metres of thread would be a

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.400
tangle that could neither fit nor be found

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.400
when needed.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.840
The first level of packing is that each

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.280
chromosome's DNA wraps around groups of proteins called

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.480
histones, like thread round a spool.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.760
DNA carries a negative charge and histones carry

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.280
a positive one, and opposite charges attract, so

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.800
the thread clings to them.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.800
One spool of DNA and histones is called

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:23.920
a nucleosome.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.280
The whole beaded thread is chromatin, and the

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.960
cell folds it further.

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:32.966
Winding creates a problem, because a gene can

00:03:33.047 --> 00:03:35.606
only be read, meaning the cell uses its

00:03:35.687 --> 00:03:38.446
instructions, when the cell can reach it.

00:03:38.727 --> 00:03:40.646
So the winding is adjustable.

00:03:40.967 --> 00:03:43.286
Chemical tags on the histones let the cell

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:45.206
loosen the thread where it needs to read

00:03:45.247 --> 00:03:47.646
a gene, and tighten it elsewhere.

00:03:48.007 --> 00:03:51.046
Some tags weaken a histone's positive charge, so

00:03:51.127 --> 00:03:53.286
it grips the DNA less tightly.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.300
Before a cell divides, the chromatin is wound

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.820
far more tightly, into the short, thick chromosomes

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.660
you saw in mitosis.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.380
That shortens it thousands of times, so the

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.860
chromosomes can be moved without tangling.

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:12.026
A eukaryotic cell keeps a little DNA outside

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:13.386
the nucleus too.

00:04:13.707 --> 00:04:17.306
Mitochondria, where cells release energy from food, each

00:04:17.387 --> 00:04:19.626
hold many copies of a small ring of

00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:23.066
DNA, and so do chloroplasts, where plants carry

00:04:23.147 --> 00:04:24.266
out photosynthesis.

00:04:24.987 --> 00:04:25.866
Why a ring?

00:04:26.347 --> 00:04:29.866
Mitochondria and chloroplasts came from bacteria that began

00:04:29.947 --> 00:04:33.146
living inside larger cells, and they still carry

00:04:33.227 --> 00:04:35.386
a bacterium's kind of DNA.

00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:38.106
In our cells, these rings are extra to

00:04:38.187 --> 00:04:40.586
the 46 chromosomes in the nucleus.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.320
Put it side by side.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.520
Bacterial DNA is usually one main ring in

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.880
the nucleoid with no membrane, coiled and held

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.200
by proteins, plus plasmids.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.960
Eukaryotic DNA is several linear chromosomes in a

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.720
nucleus, wound on histones as chromatin, plus small

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.760
rings in mitochondria and chloroplasts.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.840
Both are made of the same double helix

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.920
with the same four bases.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.840
The difference is the arrangement.

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:12.706
In an exam, a compare answer needs both

00:05:12.787 --> 00:05:14.386
sides in one sentence.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:18.306
Not just bacteria have a ring, but: bacterial

00:05:18.387 --> 00:05:22.466
DNA is a circular chromosome, whereas eukaryotic DNA

00:05:22.707 --> 00:05:24.546
is several linear chromosomes.

00:05:25.427 --> 00:05:26.986
Each row of the table can become a

00:05:27.027 --> 00:05:27.986
sentence like that.

00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:32.066
Try the packaging row: bacterial DNA is coiled

00:05:32.147 --> 00:05:35.826
and held by proteins, whereas eukaryotic DNA is

00:05:35.987 --> 00:05:38.066
wound on histones as chromatin.

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.186
Two ways to keep it straight.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.466
Karyon is Greek for a kernel, here the

00:05:44.547 --> 00:05:45.186
nucleus.

00:05:45.747 --> 00:05:49.426
Pro means before, so a prokaryote is from

00:05:49.507 --> 00:05:50.866
before the nucleus.

00:05:51.507 --> 00:05:55.026
Eu means good or well, so a eukaryote

00:05:55.107 --> 00:05:58.146
has a proper nucleus, which biologists often call

00:05:58.307 --> 00:05:59.506
a true nucleus.

00:06:00.227 --> 00:06:03.666
For shapes: bacteria use a ring, we use

00:06:03.827 --> 00:06:06.626
lines in a nucleus, but our mitochondria and

00:06:06.787 --> 00:06:10.146
chloroplasts keep a ring from their bacterial past.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:13.726
Two questions, one at a time.

00:06:14.007 --> 00:06:16.566
First, a drawing of two cells.

00:06:16.807 --> 00:06:18.166
Pause here to answer.

00:06:28.567 --> 00:06:30.366
Now a student's claim.

00:06:30.647 --> 00:06:32.086
Pause here to answer.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:42.900
One.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.460
Cell P is the prokaryote.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.420
It has no nucleus, and its main DNA

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.580
is a ring lying in a clear region

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:51.780
with no membrane.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.420
The region that holds its main DNA is

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.220
the nucleoid, and the two small rings are

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.420
plasmids.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.380
Cell Q has a nucleus holding its eight

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:02.660
chromosomes.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.940
Its other DNA is outside the nucleus: small

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.660
rings inside each mitochondrion.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:10.900
If you said that extra DNA is in

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.460
the nucleus, look again at where those rings

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:13.940
sit.

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:15.846
Two.

00:07:16.127 --> 00:07:19.486
Bacteria have no nucleus, but their DNA does

00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:20.686
not float loose.

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:23.966
It sits in the nucleoid, twisted into coils

00:07:24.047 --> 00:07:25.926
and held by proteins.

00:07:26.287 --> 00:07:29.086
And forty-six is the number of DNA molecules

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:30.766
in the nucleus of a cell that has

00:07:30.847 --> 00:07:32.886
not yet copied its DNA.

00:07:33.247 --> 00:07:35.406
A human cell also has small rings of

00:07:35.487 --> 00:07:38.926
DNA in its mitochondria, so forty-six is not

00:07:39.007 --> 00:07:40.366
all of its DNA.

00:07:40.687 --> 00:07:44.126
After copying there would be ninety-two DNA molecules,

00:07:44.247 --> 00:07:46.926
two in each of the forty-six chromosomes, but

00:07:47.007 --> 00:07:49.006
that is nuclear DNA again.

00:07:50.234 --> 00:07:53.273
So when a question shows you DNA, ask

00:07:53.354 --> 00:07:56.473
three things: is there a nucleus, is the

00:07:56.554 --> 00:07:59.433
DNA a ring or a line, and is

00:07:59.514 --> 00:08:00.953
it wound on histones?

00:08:02.074 --> 00:08:04.073
Next, we see how a cell reads one

00:08:04.474 --> 00:08:07.993
gene: copying a section of DNA into mRNA.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:10.640
Here are the key notes.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.800
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:13.680
to copy them.
